    "title": "Fastest humans have ever traveled? Shocking facts about Artemis II",
    "text": "April 10, 2026, 6:01 a.m. ETWelcome back!After a record-setting trip, the Artemis II moon mission will come to an end on Friday, April 10 with a planned splashdown off the California coast. The mission set a record for humanity's greatest distance from the Earth – 252,756 miles – and it may set another record for fastest speed ever traveled by humans when the crew reaches speeds of an estimated 25,000 mph at reentry.Over the past 10 days, the world has watched as the first humans in more than five decades undertook a journey toward Earth's closest celestial neighbor and back.As we wait for the return of the four astronauts aboard the Orion capsule, let's look back at some of the incredible facts about the mission.How hot will it get during Orion's reentry?The crew module’s heat shield will protect the spacecraft as it makes its way back through Earth’s atmosphere.Temperatures could reach upward of 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit, NASA said.How far did the Artemis II mission get from Earth?On April 6, the Artemis II crew reached their maximum distance from Earth at 252,756 miles.That sets a new record for human spaceflight, according to NASA. \"This milestone places the crew 4,111 miles farther from Earth than the Apollo 13 mission in 1970.\"To put it into perspective, the distance around the Earth at the equator is about 24,901 miles.What's the fastest speed reached during Artemis II mission?That's expected to come at reentry.Flight Director Rick Henfling said Artemis II is predicted to reach a maximum velocity of about 25,000 mph when it reenters Earth's atmosphere this afternoon. If that speed is reached, it will be the fastest that humans have ever traveled, breaking the record of 24,816.1 mph set during the Apollo 10 mission in 1969.How fast will Orion capsule be going when it splashes down off California?By the time of splashdown, the capsule should be going about 17 mph, according to NASA.How fast did the Artemis II Space Launch System go?To reach orbit, the Artemis II SLS rocket hit more than 17,000 mph. That's 22 times the speed of sound.A little perspective: At 17,000 mph, it would take 14 minutes to get from Miami to Anchorage, Alaska.How close did astronauts in Orion capsule get to moon?At their closest approach during the lunar flyby on April 6, astronauts were between 4,067 and 4,070 miles above the lunar surface.How tall was the rocket that launched Artemis II mission?The space launch system used for the Artemis II moon mission was 322 feet tall.That's shorter than the 363-foot tall Saturn V rocket used for the Apollo missions.Rounding up Artemis II mission numbersHow many astronauts: 4Length of mission: 10 daysTotal distance traveled: 685,000 milesThrust during launch: 8.8 million poundsFirsts: First crewed lunar flight in 50+ years; first female astronaut, first Black astronaut and first Canadian astronaut beyond low Earth orbitWhen will the Artemis II astronauts return to Earth?The Orion capsule will reenter Earth's atmosphere Friday, April 10, as its service module responsible for propelling and maneuvering the vehicle through space will separate and burn up.That maneuver exposes the crew module's heat shield, protecting the astronauts from the conditions the vehicle will endure.Orion will then make a parachute-assisted water landing estimated for 8:07 p.m. ET Friday, April 10, in the Pacific Ocean near California off the coast of San Diego.After the landing, the Artemis II crew will exit the vehicle onto a U.S. Navy recovery vessel.",
